# mgstock.msg - Stockholm, the gate sniper (mgstock.ssl).
# 100-109 look and shout, 110-139 floating remarks, 200+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a lean man at the gate post, nursing a rifle.}
{101}{}{You see Stockholm, the man who watches Megaton's gate.}
{102}{}{He has the patient squint of someone paid to stare at nothing. His rifle is the cleanest thing for a mile in any direction.}
{105}{}{Traveler at the gate!}

# 110-121: any day.
{110}{}{Sun's up. Dust's up. All's well.}
{111}{}{Nothing out there but dirt. Some of it's moving.}
{112}{}{I can see clear to the horizon. It isn't worth the walk.}
{113}{}{Wind's from the south. Smells like brahmin. Everything does.}
{114}{}{Gecko at four hundred yards. He's thinking it over.}
{115}{}{Best view in Megaton. Worst chair.}
{116}{}{Nine dust devils today. No, ten.}
{117}{}{Eight dollars. A man could buy a hat for that.}
{118}{}{If I squint, there's almost something worth shooting.}
{119}{}{Hey, Micky. Still with us? Good man.}
{120}{}{The robot told a radscorpion the bomb hadn't gone off. The scorpion declined to wait.}
{121}{}{Caravan's late. Caravan's always late.}

# 125-127: the bomb is disarmed.
{125}{}{Quiet town, dead bomb, loaded rifle. Can't complain.}
{126}{}{I doze better on shift now the bomb's a paperweight.}
{127}{}{They pulled the big fella's teeth. Shame. He kept the raiders polite.}

# 130-132: Simms is dead.
{130}{}{I had the gate covered. Nobody said to cover the saloon.}
{131}{}{The sheriff's boy has the hat now. It comes down over his ears.}
{132}{}{One badge in the whole town, and now we're one short.}

# 135-136: Burke got away and his hired guns are due.
{135}{}{Dust on the south trail. Too slow for a caravan.}
{136}{}{Somebody out there keeps not coming closer. I don't care for it.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{Easy. I've had you in the glass for the last half mile; if you were trouble, we wouldn't be talking. Name's Stockholm. I watch the road so the town doesn't have to.}
{201}{}{You again. You're getting easier to pick out at a distance. That's a compliment.}
{202}{}{There's the bomb tamer. They argued about a statue for you, you know. Then somebody priced the bronze.}
{203}{}{Sheriff's dead. I keep looking down at the street for the hat. Habit.}
{204}{}{Word of advice. That fella in the suit left in a hurry, and his kind don't run, they fetch. Mind the road next time you come up it.}
{205}{}{Go on. I've got all day. I've got every day.}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{What do you see from this post?}
{211}{}{What should I know about Megaton?}
{212}{}{Do you ever have to use that rifle?}
{213}{}{You sound like a man with something on his mind.}
{214}{}{You see far. Me see far too!}
{215}{}{Keep your eyes open.}
{216}{}{How is your tab at the saloon?}
{217}{}{About those eight dollars you owe.}
{218}{}{Me go now.}

# The view.
{220}{}{Dirt, mostly. Geckos. A caravan every few days. Once a man on fire, running; I never did learn the story. Whatever comes up that road, I see it before it sees the wall.}
{221}{}{And at night?}
{222}{}{At night there's a green shine on the dust over the crater. The sheriff says I imagine it. The sheriff sleeps indoors.}
{223}{}{Let me ask you something else.}

# The town.
{230}{}{A crater with a wall round it and a bomb in the middle, so mind your step. Clinic's on your right going in, Brass Lantern's on the left, saloon's at the back past the bomb. Trouble goes to the sheriff. He's the one under the hat.}
{231}{}{Where can I buy supplies?}
{232}{}{Craterside Supply. Far right corner, past the water plant. Moira's honest and her prices are fair. If she asks you to help with her book, remember you have somewhere else to be.}

# The rifle.
{240}{}{Twice this month. A radscorpion, and a man who took the gate for a suggestion. The scorpion had better manners.}

# The debt.
{250}{}{Since you ask. I owe Moriarty eight dollars. He adds a dollar a week and calls it "the vig", like it's a pet. I can't leave the post, and he won't walk this far for less than ten.}
{251}{}{Here's eight. Get clear of him.}
{252}{}{I'd help, but I'm lighter than you are.}
{253}{}{Sounds like your problem.}
{260}{}{Well now. A stranger with money and no angle. I'll send the Creel girl down with it; she only skims a nickel. Anything that follows you in off the waste gets its first bullet from me, no charge.}
{261}{}{Then we're brothers. Poor ones. Forget I mentioned it.}
{262}{}{It is. You asked, I answered. That's how talking works; I'm out of practice.}
{263}{}{Clear. Moriarty looked like I'd shot his dog. Never saw a man so sad to be paid.}
{264}{}{Stay out of debt.}

# Low Intelligence.
{270}{}{Sure you do, friend. Say, you got eight dollars? Rifle man owes bar man. Bar man is mean.}
{271}{}{Me give shinies. Bar man be nice now.}
{272}{}{Me got no shinies for you.}
{273}{}{I'll be damned. Thank you. Don't tell anybody, but you're the sharpest soul I've talked to all day.}
{274}{}{There's my banker. You see anything with more than four legs out there, you wave. Deal?}
